            <ArticleTitle>Laparoscopic common bile duct exploration versus endoscopic retrograde cholangiopancreatography for choledocholithiasis found at time of laparoscopic cholecystectomy: Analysis of a large integrated health care system database.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1075-1079</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0002-9610(17)30469-5</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.amjsurg.2017.08.030</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">We compared endoscopic retrograde cholangiopancreatography (ERCP) and laparoscopic common bile duct exploration (LCBDE) for managing choledocholithiasis found at time of cholecystectomy.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">One hundred and five LCBDE (2005-2015) were compared to 195 LC/ERCP (2014-2015) from the Southern California Kaiser Permanente database.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">LC/ERCP was more effective at clearing the CBD (98% vs. 88.6%, p = 0.01); but required more procedures per patient (mean ± standard deviation, 1.1 ± 0.4 vs. 2.0 ± 0.12, p &lt; 0.001). Morbidity, hospital length of stay and readmission were not different (P &gt; 0.05). Four patients failed ERCP, while 12 patients failed LCBDE and had subsequent ERCP (10) or CBD exploration (2). All patients with RYGB had successful LCBDE.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">LC/ERCP is better than LCBDE in clearing CBD stones, but has similar morbidity and is an effective alternative for patients with RYGB.</AbstractText>
